# {{ project_name }}

Deployable OpenELIS distribution for {{ context_name }}.

This repo was generated from
[openelis-distro-template](https://github.com/DIGI-UW/openelis-distro-template)
via `copier copy --trust`. See `.copier-answers.yml` for the answers used.
To pull template improvements later, run `copier update` from this repo.

## Quickstart

```bash
cp .env.example .env
docker compose up -d
```

Open `http://localhost:8080/OpenELIS-Global/` once the stack is up.

`.env.example` ships with secrets generated at template-render time
(unique to this distro). They flow straight into the demo stack so the
quickstart boots without further edits.

## Contextualization

Start from the demo defaults, then replace them with deployment-specific
values:

```bash
python3 tools/contextualize/oe_context.py doctor
python3 tools/contextualize/oe_context.py apply --profile demo --dry-run
python3 tools/contextualize/oe_context.py apply --profile demo --non-interactive
```

The walkthrough in [docs/contextualization.md](docs/contextualization.md)
follows the same order as `context/config-surfaces.yml`, which is also
the manifest used by the CLI.

## Pre-production gate

Before going to production, rotate the generated secrets in `.env` and
confirm:

```bash
python3 tools/contextualize/oe_context.py doctor --prod
```

This fails if `.env` still ships with the `.env.example` defaults or
any known-bad upstream literals.

## Analyzer profiles

Vendor profiles ship under `configs/analyzer-profiles/core/`. Drop
distro-specific overrides or additions into
`configs/analyzer-profiles/distro/<protocol>/` and re-run `oe_context.py
apply`. See [docs/analyzer-profiles.md](docs/analyzer-profiles.md).

## Cutting a release

`gh workflow run release.yml -f distro_version=X.Y.Z -f oe_version=... -f
bridge_version=...` builds a tarball, rewrites image-tag pins, and
publishes a draft GitHub Release. See
[docs/release-workflow.md](docs/release-workflow.md).
